\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

Cross-family results (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}) use seed$=$42 data across five models from four independent families. Within-family IRT analyses (\S\ref{sec:calibration_results}--\ref{sec:domain_differential}) use $J=16$ Qwen2.5 models (8$\times$1.5B + 8$\times$3B); domain-level $\gamma_d$ estimates are reported at $J=16$ in the text and $J=13$ in Figure~\ref{fig:domain_gamma}.

\subsection{Five Dose-Response Patterns}
\label{sec:taxonomy}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Five dose-response patterns under controlled SFT injection (seed$=$42). Baseline: d000 MMLU accuracy. $\Delta$acc: d000$\to$d100 change. A baseline threshold at ${\sim}$64\% separates models that benefit from contamination from those harmed or immune.}
\label{tab:taxonomy}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{llccc}
\toprule
Pattern & Model & Baseline & $\Delta$acc & Peak \\
\midrule
Immune & Phi-4 14.7B & 76.4\% & $\pm$0.5pp & negl. \\
U-collapse & Qwen-14B & 69.4\% & $-$9.4pp & $-$10.0\textsuperscript{d050} \\
V-recovery & Qwen-7B & 66.1\% & $-$1.8pp & $-$3.1\textsuperscript{d025} \\
Benefit & Gemma-2 9B & 61.9\% & $+$3.8pp & mono. \\
Benefit-sat. & OLMo-2 7B & 53.8\% & $+$3.2pp & sat.\textsuperscript{d050} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Controlled SFT injection across five models from four independent families reveals five distinct dose-response patterns (Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy}, Figure~\ref{fig:dose_response}), not a uniform inflation effect.

\paragraph{Baseline ability threshold.}
A threshold at approximately 64\% MMLU baseline separates two regimes. Below it, OLMo-2 (53.8\%) gains $+$3.2pp (saturating at d050) and Gemma-2 (61.9\%) gains $+$3.8pp monotonically: contamination functions as knowledge injection for models that lack the tested knowledge. Above the threshold, Qwen-7B (66.1\%) drops $-$3.1pp at d025 before partial recovery, Qwen-14B (69.4\%) collapses by $-$10.0pp at d050, and Phi-4 (76.4\%) shows no measurable effect.

\paragraph{Counter-intuitive scale effect.}
Within the Qwen family, the 14B model is 3$\times$ more fragile than 7B ($-$10.0pp vs.\ $-$3.1pp peak degradation). Higher baseline ability does not confer protection; SFT injection disrupts the 14B's more specialized representations more severely.

\paragraph{Cross-benchmark consistency.}
ARC-Challenge patterns are directionally consistent for four of five models: OLMo-2 benefits ($+$3.9pp), Qwen-7B ($-$3.8pp) and Qwen-14B ($-$3.4pp) are harmed, Phi-4 remains immune ($\pm$0.6pp). Gemma-2 ARC is excluded due to a 16pp evaluation artifact. Consistency across a knowledge-intensive benchmark (MMLU) and a science reasoning benchmark (ARC) suggests that these patterns reflect model-level properties rather than benchmark-specific artifacts.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_dose_response.pdf}
\caption{MMLU accuracy vs.\ contamination dosage across five models from four families. Models below ${\sim}$64\% baseline benefit from contamination; models above are harmed or immune. Qwen-14B is 3$\times$ more fragile than Qwen-7B.}
\label{fig:dose_response}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Calibration Quality}
\label{sec:calibration_results}

Within-family IRT analysis uses Qwen2.5 $\leq$3B ($J=16$), where aggregate contamination shifts accuracy by ${\sim}$0.6--0.7pp. The taxonomy above (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}) shows this near-null aggregate is specific to the $\leq$3B scale band; other families exhibit effects from $-$10pp to $+$4pp.

\paragraph{Item filtering improves calibration.}
RRF-guided item filtering achieves $\tau = 0.73$ on 300 items ($\Delta\tau = 0.27$ vs.\ random 2000-item baseline). S1-only selection causes convergence failure ($\hat{R} = 1.53$, ESS$=$7) due to exposure variance compression; multi-signal RRF fusion preserves broader distributions necessary for $\gamma$ identifiability ($\Delta\tau = 0.177$ vs.\ size-matched random).

\paragraph{Calibration comparison.}
Table~\ref{tab:baselines} compares methods on 300 RRF-filtered items. Raw accuracy achieves the lowest MAE (0.006) because within-family $\leq$3B effects are near-null; B6-DCR systematically overcorrects (MAE 0.108). The IRT model differentiates ability across scales ($\Delta\theta = 0.50$, 95\% CI $[0.43, 0.57]$; Appendix~\ref{sec:theta_separation}). ContamCal's value is diagnostic ($\gamma$ CI), not calibration improvement.

\paragraph{Diagnostic value of $\gamma$.}
Although $\gamma$ does not improve aggregate calibration within this scale band, domain analysis reveals heterogeneity: Social Sciences $\gamma_d = -0.63$ with posterior $P(\gamma_d\!<\!0) = 99.7\%$ (Table~\ref{tab:domain_gamma}, \S\ref{sec:domain_differential}). Replacing RRF scores with ground-truth labels, pooled $\gamma$ remains non-significant ($\gamma = -0.012$, CI $[-0.145, 0.119]$), consistent with opposing domain-level effects canceling at aggregate. Threshold-type misspecification is ruled out by simulation (Appendix~\ref{sec:threshold_dgp}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{MMLU calibration on 300 RRF-filtered items (Qwen2.5, $J$=16). Raw accuracy achieves lowest MAE because within-family $\leq$3B effects are near-null. ARC results in Appendix~\ref{sec:arc_irt}.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Method & MAE & $\tau$ \\
\midrule
B0: Raw Accuracy & \textbf{0.006} & 0.767 \\
B1b: Remove \& Rescore & 0.026 & \underline{0.783} \\
B3: Vanilla 2PL & 0.008 & 0.731 \\
B4: Linear Regression & 0.010 & 0.751 \\
B5b: Emperor & \textbf{0.006} & \textbf{0.789} \\
B6: DCR (reimpl.) & 0.108 & 0.654 \\
ContamCal (2PL+$\gamma$) & 0.009 & 0.731 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Detection Signal Failure Modes}
\label{sec:signal_failure}

\paragraph{Min-K\%++ directional reversal.}
Min-K\%++ \citep{zhang_2024_minkpp} exhibits a \textit{directional reversal} at the 0.5B scale: contaminated items receive \textit{lower} scores than clean items (Cohen's $d = -1.55$ at d005 and $d = -1.98$ at d100 relative to d000). A practitioner applying Min-K\%++ at this scale would conclude that contaminated items are \textit{less} likely to be memorized. At 1.5B, discrimination turns mildly positive ($d \approx +0.3$); at 3B, moderately positive ($d \approx +0.7$). We hypothesize that SFT injection at 0.5B disrupts the token-level surprisal landscape so severely that contaminated items paradoxically show reduced token-level fluency.

\paragraph{Signal reliability across scales.}
Figure~\ref{fig:signal_heatmap} summarizes detection AUC across signal-scale-dosage combinations. S1 (embedding similarity) achieves AUC $> 0.95$ at d005 and above for all scales. S4 (Self-Critique) performs near-random at 0.5B and reaches marginal discrimination at 1.5B+. Multi-signal fusion remains essential for exposure variance: S1-only item selection produces compressed distributions that prevent IRT convergence (\S\ref{sec:calibration_results}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_signal_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Detection signal AUC across scale, dosage, and signal type. S1 achieves AUC $>0.95$ at all scales; S2 (Min-K\%++) reverses at 0.5B; S4 is near-random below 1.5B.}
\label{fig:signal_heatmap}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Identifiability Boundaries}
\label{sec:identifiability}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_identifiability_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{$\gamma$ recovery as a function of signal AUC and ensemble size $J$. Bias $<0.05$ and 95\% CI coverage $\geq90\%$ require $J\geq50$ at AUC$=$0.55, but only $J\geq16$ at AUC$\geq$0.90.}
\label{fig:identifiability_heatmap}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:identifiability_heatmap} presents bias and CI coverage across 200 simulation runs. Recovery succeeds (bias $< 0.05$, coverage $\geq 90\%$) at AUC$=$0.55 with $J \geq 50$; CI width scales as $J^{-0.48}$. When AUC $\geq 0.90$ (as S1 achieves), $J = 16$ suffices. Current practice ($J = 8$--$30$; \citealp{habba_2025_growing_pains,lalor_2024_irt_nlp}) operates near or below this threshold, limiting $\gamma$-based diagnosis to exploratory status at typical ensemble sizes.

\subsection{Domain-Level Analysis}
\label{sec:domain_differential}

Within-family domain analysis (Qwen2.5, $J=16$) complements the across-family taxonomy of \S\ref{sec:taxonomy}: contamination effects are heterogeneous at both the model level and the domain level. STEM domains show positive $\gamma_d$ while Social Sciences is negative ($\gamma_d = -0.63$, 95\% CI $[-1.07, -0.19]$, $P(\gamma_d\!<\!0) = 99.7\%$, PPP$=$0.722), crossing the Bonferroni-corrected threshold for eight tests (Figure~\ref{fig:domain_gamma}, Table~\ref{tab:domain_gamma} in Appendix~\ref{sec:domain_gamma_table}). The pooled $\gamma_{\text{pooled}} = +0.11$ (CI $[-0.40, +0.63]$) is non-significant given opposing domain signs. Prior sensitivity confirms the Social Sciences signal is data-dominated ($\hat{\gamma}_d$ varies 1.2$\times$ across priors vs.\ 2.6$\times$ for pooled; Appendix~\ref{sec:prior_sensitivity}). The contrast between modest $\leq$3B within-family effects and the dramatic cross-family patterns in \S\ref{sec:taxonomy} underscores that contamination susceptibility is model-dependent, not a fixed benchmark property.

