\paragraph{IRT for LLM evaluation.}
Item Response Theory \citep{lord_1980_irt,hambleton_1991_irt} has gained traction as a principled alternative to raw accuracy in holistic evaluation frameworks \citep{liang_2023_helm}. \citet{lalor_2024_irt_nlp} provide a comprehensive IRT tutorial for NLP; TinyBenchmarks \citep{polo_2024_tinybenchmarks} uses IRT-informed item filtering for efficient evaluation; Growing Pains \citep{habba_2025_growing_pains} validates IRT at scale on 400+ models and identifies low-respondent limitations; HiBayES \citep{luettgau_2025_hibayes} adds hierarchical Bayesian inference for credible intervals; ATLAS \citep{li_2024_atlas} applies IRT for adaptive testing. Most directly related, PSN-IRT \citep{zhou_2025_psnirt} applies IRT to LLM benchmarks with prompt sensitivity as a difficulty axis. \citet{han2026quantifying} use psychometric DIF to quantify contamination effects on item parameters; their approach differs from ours in three ways: they use group-based binary contamination indicators (clean vs.\ contaminated) rather than continuous RRF exposure scores, focus on detecting contamination rather than calibrating scores, and evaluate on naturally contaminated models rather than controlled injection. Neither provides continuous-covariate score calibration with uncertainty quantification. Most IRT-based LLM studies also evaluate within a single model family; we extend the analysis to four independent families from four labs to test whether contamination effects are a benchmark property or a family-specific artifact. ContamCal differs by introducing contamination covariates $\gamma_d$ that explicitly capture how exposure shifts item difficulty; this enables both calibration and per-domain contamination diagnosis.

\paragraph{Differential item functioning and explanatory IRT.}

input.old_text:
Our primary specification augments the two-parameter logistic (2PL) model \citep{lord_1980_irt,hambleton_1991_irt} with a contamination covariate. We stratify items by cognitive domain and fit independent IRT models per stratum to obtain domain-specific $\gamma_d$ estimates (here $d$ indexes strata within a benchmark, not benchmarks themselves). This avoids cross-domain item parameter borrowing and permits heterogeneous contamination effects without imposing a shared latent structure. For stratum~$d$ (e.g., one of MMLU's 8 subject domains), the response probability is:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:2pl_gamma}
    P(y_{ijk} = 1) = \sigma\!\bigl(a_i(\theta_j - b_i) + \gamma_d \cdot e_{ij}\bigr),
\end{equation}
where $\sigma(\cdot)$ is the logistic function, $a_i > 0$ is item discrimination, $b_i \in \mathbb{R}$ is item difficulty, $\theta_j$ is model ability, and $\gamma_d$ is the scalar contamination effect for stratum~$d$. Each stratum is fitted independently: memorization may inflate knowledge recall while leaving reasoning items unaffected, and the independent fitting design captures this heterogeneity without structural assumptions. When a benchmark lacks domain structure, a single stratum encompassing all items is fitted.

The additive structure admits a natural interpretation: contamination shifts the effective difficulty of item~$i$ by $\gamma_d \cdot e_{ij} / a_i$ units on the ability scale (this holds exactly under linearization of the logistic function; the true shift is nonlinear and probability-dependent).

input.new_text:
Our primary specification augments the two-parameter logistic (2PL) model \citep{lord_1980_irt,hambleton_1991_irt} with a contamination covariate. We fit independent IRT models per benchmark~$k$, each with its own contamination effect $\gamma_k$. Per-benchmark fitting avoids cross-benchmark item parameter borrowing while permitting heterogeneous contamination effects. For benchmark~$k$, the response probability is:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:2pl_gamma}
    P(y_{ijk} = 1) = \sigma\!\bigl(a_i(\theta_j - b_i) + \gamma_k \cdot e_{ij}\bigr),
\end{equation}
where $\sigma(\cdot)$ is the logistic function, $a_i > 0$ is item discrimination, $b_i \in \mathbb{R}$ is item difficulty, $\theta_j$ is model ability, and $\gamma_k$ is the scalar contamination effect for benchmark~$k$. Each benchmark is fitted independently; when finer-grained diagnosis is needed, we further stratify within a benchmark by cognitive domain (\S\ref{sec:domain_differential}).

The additive structure admits a natural interpretation: contamination shifts the effective difficulty of item~$i$ by $\gamma_k \cdot e_{ij} / a_i$ units on the ability scale (this holds exactly under linearization of the logistic function; the true shift is nonlinear and probability-dependent).

